WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:05.011
In this lesson you'll learn how SynthID text

2
00:00:05.011 --> 00:00:08.389
hides a watermark in an AI's word choices.

3
00:00:08.389 --> 00:00:10.909
Nothing is added to the text.

4
00:00:10.909 --> 00:00:14.775
Instead, it tilts which word gets picked,

5
00:00:14.775 --> 00:00:18.130
and only when the model had a real choice.

6
00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:20.521
So the writing reads the same,

7
00:00:20.521 --> 00:00:23.761
exact answers stay exact, and anyone holding

8
00:00:23.761 --> 00:00:27.688
the secret key can check for the mark.

9
00:00:27.688 --> 00:00:31.322
Say the model is finishing this sentence:

10
00:00:31.322 --> 00:00:33.841
the tea house sits at the edge of the blank.

11
00:00:33.841 --> 00:00:35.896
It has its odds.

12
00:00:35.896 --> 00:00:38.497
Garden, thirty-four percent.

13
00:00:38.497 --> 00:00:39.832
River, twenty.

14
00:00:39.832 --> 00:00:41.527
Forest, sixteen.

15
00:00:41.527 --> 00:00:43.013
And a few more.

16
00:00:43.013 --> 00:00:45.847
Normally a random number would pick one.

17
00:00:46.275 --> 00:00:50.652
SynthID runs a tournament instead.

18
00:00:50.652 --> 00:00:52.010
It fills the bracket

19
00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.704
with contestants drawn from those odds,

20
00:00:54.704 --> 00:00:57.421
so likely words get more contestants.

21
00:00:57.421 --> 00:01:00.335
Before the first match, every possible word

22
00:01:00.335 --> 00:01:03.563
gets a plus or a minus for each round.

23
00:01:03.563 --> 00:01:05.722
Those are its round assignments.

24
00:01:05.722 --> 00:01:07.905
They come from the secret key

25
00:01:07.905 --> 00:01:10.378
and the four tokens before the blank,

26
00:01:10.378 --> 00:01:13.872
so the same key and the same four tokens

27
00:01:13.872 --> 00:01:16.275
always give the same assignments.

28
00:01:16.275 --> 00:01:20.119
In each match, a plus beats a minus.

29
00:01:20.119 --> 00:01:24.333
Two pluses, or two minuses, and it's a coin flip.

30
00:01:24.333 --> 00:01:26.574
The last contestant standing

31
00:01:26.574 --> 00:01:28.187
is the word that gets written.

32
00:01:28.187 --> 00:01:31.322
Here, river had the better assignments,

33
00:01:31.322 --> 00:01:33.915
and river won.

34
00:01:33.915 --> 00:01:35.575
With these four tokens,

35
00:01:35.575 --> 00:01:37.735
the assignments favour river.

36
00:01:37.735 --> 00:01:40.010
Change the tokens before the blank,

37
00:01:40.010 --> 00:01:42.448
and every assignment is redrawn.

38
00:01:42.448 --> 00:01:44.062
Now garden is favoured.

39
00:01:44.062 --> 00:01:45.548
Now forest.

40
00:01:45.548 --> 00:01:48.508
Across thousands of different sentences,

41
00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:50.041
each word still wins

42
00:01:50.041 --> 00:01:52.711
about as often as the model wanted.

43
00:01:52.711 --> 00:01:55.463
The real system plays thirty rounds,

44
00:01:55.463 --> 00:01:57.495
not three, so in any one spot

45
00:01:57.495 --> 00:02:00.467
the assignments nearly decide the word.

46
00:02:00.467 --> 00:02:03.149
But averaged over everything the model writes,

47
00:02:03.149 --> 00:02:04.716
the odds are its own.

48
00:02:04.716 --> 00:02:07.495
That's why the quality holds.

49
00:02:07.495 --> 00:02:10.689
Now ask: what's the capital of France?

50
00:02:11.152 --> 00:02:13.857
The model is almost certain it's Paris.

51
00:02:13.857 --> 00:02:16.760
So almost every contestant is Paris.

52
00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.395
Paris plays Paris, and Paris wins,

53
00:02:19.395 --> 00:02:21.522
whatever the assignments say.

54
00:02:21.984 --> 00:02:24.481
The watermark had nothing to act on,

55
00:02:24.481 --> 00:02:27.163
so the answer stays exact.

56
00:02:27.163 --> 00:02:30.495
The same goes for a web address, a file path,

57
00:02:30.495 --> 00:02:34.129
or a quote you asked for word for word.

58
00:02:34.129 --> 00:02:37.124
But ask for a city famous for its food,

59
00:02:37.124 --> 00:02:40.572
and Paris is one good answer among many.

60
00:02:40.572 --> 00:02:42.314
Now the assignments matter,

61
00:02:42.314 --> 00:02:45.739
and the watermark does its usual work.

62
00:02:45.739 --> 00:02:47.445
It's not about the word.

63
00:02:47.445 --> 00:02:51.105
It's about whether there was a choice.

64
00:02:51.105 --> 00:02:53.729
The checker never needs the model.

65
00:02:53.729 --> 00:02:56.678
It reads the text one token at a time.

66
00:02:56.678 --> 00:02:58.570
For each token, it takes the four

67
00:02:58.570 --> 00:03:01.844
tokens before it, adds the secret key,

68
00:03:01.844 --> 00:03:05.676
and recreates that token's round assignments.

69
00:03:05.676 --> 00:03:08.265
Then it counts the pluses.

70
00:03:08.265 --> 00:03:10.958
Text written without the watermark

71
00:03:10.958 --> 00:03:13.350
lands near half pluses.

72
00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:16.577
Watermarked text leans toward plus,

73
00:03:16.577 --> 00:03:18.133
and the longer the text,

74
00:03:18.133 --> 00:03:20.420
the less that lean can be luck.

75
00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:21.721
Check with the wrong key,

76
00:03:21.721 --> 00:03:25.006
and the assignments are just noise.

77
00:03:25.006 --> 00:03:26.364
Back to half.

78
00:03:26.364 --> 00:03:28.907
That's why only the key holder can check.

79
00:03:28.907 --> 00:03:30.544
And a hit only says the model

80
00:03:30.544 --> 00:03:32.216
was probably involved.

81
00:03:32.216 --> 00:03:35.505
It can't say who wrote the text.

82
00:03:35.505 --> 00:03:37.084
Change one token,

83
00:03:37.084 --> 00:03:40.288
and five tokens get new assignments:

84
00:03:40.288 --> 00:03:42.877
that one, and the four after it,

85
00:03:42.877 --> 00:03:46.035
because their previous tokens changed.

86
00:03:46.035 --> 00:03:48.148
Their evidence is gone.

87
00:03:48.148 --> 00:03:51.155
Every other token keeps its evidence.

88
00:03:51.155 --> 00:03:54.429
Swapping in synonyms does the most damage,

89
00:03:54.429 --> 00:03:57.389
because synonyms are exactly the free choices

90
00:03:57.389 --> 00:03:59.711
where the watermark lives.

91
00:03:59.711 --> 00:04:03.055
Asking the same model to reword the text

92
00:04:03.055 --> 00:04:06.689
just writes new watermarked text.

93
00:04:06.689 --> 00:04:10.056
A rewrite by a model without the key picks

94
00:04:10.056 --> 00:04:13.775
every word again, and the watermark is gone.

95
00:04:13.775 --> 00:04:16.619
So length is everything.

96
00:04:16.619 --> 00:04:20.555
A short email can lose its mark to a few edits.

97
00:04:20.555 --> 00:04:22.982
A whole book keeps it through a lot of them.

98
00:04:22.982 --> 00:04:24.595
Three things to keep.

99
00:04:24.595 --> 00:04:28.706
The watermark lives in free word choices.

100
00:04:28.706 --> 00:04:31.051
Exact answers stay exact,

101
00:04:31.051 --> 00:04:33.721
because there was nothing to choose.

102
00:04:33.721 --> 00:04:36.136
And the checker counts pluses,

103
00:04:36.136 --> 00:04:39.991
so long text keeps the mark, and a rewrite

104
00:04:39.991 --> 00:04:43.057
by a model without the key removes it.
